#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The supervisor of a project's orchestrator session.

usage: jig orch <project> start | stop | status | events [--old | --next [<ID>[:<minutes>] ...]]
       | rollover
       jig orch <project> wait-on [<other> <what>... | <other> --clear]

  start   refuses when impl/orch/ORCHESTRATOR.md is not in the project's clone, when a
          loop already runs, or when tmux session <project>-orch exists and is not the
          loop's own or has a live pane; else it makes ~/ws/reviews/<project>/orch/ when
          it is missing and starts the loop in the background
  stop    ends the loop and the Claude Code session that the loop started, and leaves
          the tmux session with its dead pane; a session that is not the loop's own is
          left as it is
  status  exit 0 when a loop runs, 3 when none does; with the state of the session's
          pane (live, dead or none), whether the session is the loop's own, and the
          last line of its log; then a line with the activity state of the project and
          its since, and a line for each wait: the other project, its state and since,
          and what is waited for; last the line "waiting: " with the line of
          orch/waiting, or "waiting: none" when that file is missing
  events  the session's watcher on the event file, orch/events.log: prints each line
          after the last line "start <session ID>", or from the top when there is none,
          then each new line as it is appended, and runs until it is stopped, its
          parent process ends or its output is closed; a missing file is waited for
  events --old
          prints once the events of the session before the newest one, the lines
          between the last two start lines, and exits 0; with one start line, the
          lines before it; with none, or with no file, nothing
  events --next [<ID>[:<minutes>] ...]
          the session's watcher that ends at the first event: prints the lines of
          the event file after its mark, orch/events.mark, leaving out the lines
          "hourly update", moves the mark past them and exits 0. Lines "hourly update"
          alone move the mark and do not end it; a "start <session ID>" line is an
          event like any other, so a watcher left over from an ended session stops
          at the start of the next one. The mark is the number of lines already
          printed; with no mark file, a mark file that does not hold one whole
          number, or a mark beyond the end of the file, it is the line number of the
          last "start <session ID>" line, or 0 with none.
          For each <ID>, it also runs jig wait <project> <ID> as its child, with
          --expected <minutes> when the ID has them; minutes that are not a whole
          number above 0 are a usage error, and no child starts. When a wait ends
          other than at the unit's report commit, it prints the whole output of that
          wait, then "jig wait <ID> exit <code>: <the wait's last line of output>",
          and exits 0; at the report commit it prints nothing for that wait and goes
          on, because the report line of the event file tells the news. Each child
          keeps its own output. The children end with the command, also at SIGTERM,
          SIGHUP or SIGINT. It also ends, with no line, when its parent process ends
  rollover
          run by the session itself to end its own Claude Code session, so that the
          loop starts the next one as on any exit; first stop every background shell
          of the session and its watcher. It sends SIGTERM to the pane's process only,
          prints one line before the signal and does not wait. It refuses, and signals
          nothing, when no loop runs, when the session is not live or not the loop's
          own, when the pane's process does not run with the --session-id in
          orch/session-ids, or when the caller does not run inside that pane
  wait-on <other> <what>...
          writes the line of <other> in orch/waits: the project waits on <other> for
          <what>; the since of the line is kept when the line holds the same <what>.
          It refuses when ~/ws/reviews/<project>/orch/, or ~/ws/reviews/<other>/orch/
          in the home of the orchestrator account, does not exist, and makes
          neither; it warns when
          the activity file of <other> is missing or stale: its loop then writes
          none, because it runs a jig older than 0.8.0 or no loop runs
  wait-on <other> --clear
          removes the line of <other>; exit 0 also when there was none
  wait-on prints each wait, with the activity state of <other> and its since, or
          "no waits"

  1  a refusal; 2 a usage error

The loop starts Claude Code in tmux <project>-orch, Opus at high effort, with
ORCHESTRATOR.md as its first message, --remote-control <project>-orch and the
orchestrator's directories as --add-dir. When the session exits, the loop copies its
transcript and starts it again; the third exit in 10 minutes stops the loop instead.

The session's folders. Before each session, the first one at start and each one after
an exit, the folders are read again: the orchestrator's list of lib/jig_dirs.py and
the orchestrator's folders in the section Directories of the kickoff record at the
newest kickoff tag on origin; each that exists is passed. A line of that section that
jig cannot read is ignored, with one line on stderr at start and in the log in the
loop. A folder outside the approved list of the orchestrator account is refused with
one line: start then starts no loop and no session, and the loop starts no session,
writes the line to the log and stops. Then the -dev clone and each folder passed get
the trust mark of Claude Code in ~/.claude.json, by lib/jig_trust.py, all in one read
and one rename of the file, with one line
"trust <account> <folder>: trusted" or "not marked" for each mark set or not set, on
stdout at start and in the log in the loop; a mark that is there gives no line.

The session's pane. A person's tmux client attached to the session stays attached across
each restart and each stop, because the session itself never ends: the loop sets the
window option remain-on-exit, so that the pane stays, dead, when Claude Code exits. The
session is live when its pane is not dead; a session that exists can be down. When the
session does not exist, the loop makes it with tmux new-session; when it exists with a
dead pane, the loop starts the next Claude Code in that pane with tmux respawn-pane -k,
and the tee goes on. Each start gives Claude Code a new --session-id, and the loop then
writes the tmux session ID and that Claude Code session ID to orch/session-ids. The
session is the loop's own only when both match that file: the tmux ID of the session,
and the ID after --session-id in the pane's start command. The loop tests this before
it respawns the pane, and stops, with a line in its log, when the session is not its
own. On a stop the loop sends SIGTERM to the pane's process, Claude Code, and SIGKILL
10 s later when it still runs; stop does the same when no loop did it. A session that
is not the loop's own is never touched, and the third exit in 10 minutes leaves the
dead pane for the next start.

Forks of the old session. A session that exits while it still has background work can
be carried on by Claude Code with Remote Control in a background process, started with
--fork-session --resume and the old transcript; that process would act on the project
beside the new session. So when the session exits, and for JIG_ORCH_FORK_WINDOW seconds
after the new session starts, the loop sends SIGTERM to each process of its own account
that has the argument --resume followed by the old session's ID or by a path that ends
in /<ID>.jsonl, and logs one line with its pid and the old ID. A process that ends
before the signal is no failure. It touches no other process, also none that resumes
another session. When the loop itself ends (a stop, the third exit in 10 minutes, or a
session that it may not start), it writes its state down, looks for forks of its last
session at once and once more JIG_ORCH_FORK_WINDOW seconds later, and only then frees
its lock. So jig orch stop waits for the lock that much longer before it sends SIGKILL;
when no loop runs, stop takes the same two looks itself, after it has ended Claude Code.

New files. The loop appends one event for each new file in
<root>/in/<project>/ (inbox: <file> (<bytes>, sha256 <8 hex>)) and for each readback, the
output file that jig run writes for a block, in <root>/out/<project>/ (readback: <ID>).
A line goes out once the file has held still for one poll. A file is new when its path,
inode or modification time differs from those of the file last announced at that path:
a readback written again gets a second line, an inbox file that grew may get one, and a
file removed and written again is new. orch/announced keeps the path, inode and
modification time of each file whose line is in the event file, so a file that lands
while no loop runs, or whose line a stop dropped from the queue, is announced at the
next start. At the first start, with no record yet, the files already
there are taken as announced. A record line of the older form, a path alone, stands for
the file that is at that path now, so an upgrade announces nothing again.

Reports. Every JIG_ORCH_REPORT_POLL seconds the loop reads BRANCH from the conf file
again and fetches that branch from origin of the project's clone, into
refs/jig-orch/<BRANCH> only, and appends the event "report: <ID> <status>" for each new commit whose subject is exactly "log: <ID>",
the commit that ends a unit. The status is the text before the first colon of line 1 of
impl/log/<ID>-report.md at that commit (the whole line when it has no colon), read as
jig close reads it, or "unreadable" when the file cannot be read. The fetch moves no
other ref and writes no FETCH_HEAD, so it never disturbs a fetch of the same clone by
jig wait, jig batch or a person. A fetch that fails, or that takes longer than 30 s (or
the report poll, when that is shorter), is logged once for each run of failures and
tried again at the next poll. orch/reported keeps the commits announced, each written
there once its line is appended, so each gets one line across restarts. With no record
yet, the first fetch that works takes the log commits already on origin as announced,
except those whose commit time is at or after the loop's start, so a report that lands
while the first fetches fail is not lost. When BRANCH changed since the last poll, as
when the project moves to a new version line while the loop runs, the loop logs one
line that names the old and the new branch. The first fetch of the new branch that
works then takes as announced each log commit that the old branch also held, as last
fetched, and each log commit made before the loop's start; a log commit made after the
start that only the new branch holds gets its line. A conf file that cannot be read
keeps the branch of the last poll, with one line in the log for each run of failures.

Hourly. Every JIG_ORCH_HOURLY seconds the loop ticks. The tick never wakes the model:
the orchestrator writes its updates when a result changes what Eric expects, not by the
clock, so the tick appends no event of its own. When something changed since the last
tick, the tick writes nothing, because the hour was not idle. When nothing changed, it
appends one idle line to impl/orch/updates.log in the project's clone, so that a quiet
hour still leaves a mark in the log. Something changed when the loop appended an
inbox, readback or report line to the event file (a line still waiting in the queue
and the reminders do not count), or when refs/jig-orch/<BRANCH> moved and git diff
--name-only from its last position names a file other than impl/orch/updates.log. The
first fetch that works only sets that position, as it sets the report record, and so
does the first fetch of a new branch; a failed fetch changes nothing. The idle line reads

  <date -Iseconds> idle: waits for <what> since <time>

where <what> is "unit <ID>" when the newest line of impl/log/status.log at the fetched
ref starts a unit ("<time> <ID> start started <commit>"; jig close ends it with
"<time> <ID> end <status> <commit>"), and "the orchestrator" otherwise; <time> is the
last change, or the start of the loop when there was none. The loop only appends to
the file and never commits it: the orchestrator commits it with its next commit, and
jig start, jig close and jig report work beside the uncommitted change.

Reminders. At its start and at each hourly tick the loop reads impl/orch/state.md in the
project's clone, the working tree and not a commit, and looks at each list item under
the heading "## Open". An item whose text holds "act by <YYYY-MM-DD>" (the last such date
counts) with that date today or earlier gets one line "reminder: <the item>", its lines
joined into one. orch/reminded keeps each item text with its date once its line is
appended, so neither a later tick nor a restart appends it again; a new date, or a new
text, appends it once more. A reminder line does not count as a change for the hourly tick. A
state.md that cannot be read is logged once until it can be read again.

Delivery. The loop types nothing into the session: each event is one line appended to
the event file, orch/events.log, which the session follows with jig orch <project>
events. Before it starts a session the loop appends "start <session ID>", with the ID
that it gives Claude Code by --session-id, so that a person or a check can find the
session's transcript by it. The lines after the last start line are then the events of
the running session, and those between the last two start lines the events of the
session before it, among them any that came while that session ended. When the start
line cannot be appended, the session is not started, because its watcher would show it
the events of the old session as new; that counts as an exit. Every event waits in
one queue, and the events go in the order they came, each once. An append that fails
leaves its line at the head of the queue, so that no later line overtakes it; it is
logged once, to the tee and the log, and tried again at the next poll. Events are
appended also while a dialog shows. A dialog in the session (no input bar for a few
polls) is reported at once, in one line to the tee and to the log, and never answered.
JIG_SRV names <root> (/srv/jig); JIG_ORCH_POLL the seconds between polls (5);
JIG_ORCH_REPORT_POLL the seconds between fetches (60); JIG_ORCH_HOURLY the seconds
between ticks (3600); JIG_ORCH_FORK_WINDOW the seconds after a restart in which the
loop still looks for forks of the old session (60); JIG_ORCH_ACTIVITY the seconds
between activity polls (60); JIG_ORCH_STALL_SECONDS the stall time in seconds, in
place of ORCH_STALL_MINUTES; JIG_ORCH_WAIT the command that events --next runs as
jig wait, with <project> <ID> as its arguments (jig-wait of this tree).

Activity. Once an activity poll, the loop writes its project's state to orch/activity:
one line of three fields split by tabs, the state, since and written, the times in
date -Iseconds form, by a temporary file and a rename. The state is the first that
holds of: unit, a lock in the lock directory (JIG_LOCK_DIR, /run/jig/lock) named
<project>-<account> for an account of the conf file, younger than 3 times the
expected minutes beside it; down, the session is not live; idle, a dialog (no input
bar for a few polls); busy, a spinner line of a running turn just above the input box;
idle, else. The accounts of the conf file include the orchestrator account: the
account that ORCH_ACCOUNT names, or orchestrator when the file has no such key.
since is when the state began, kept across a restart of the loop when the
old file holds the same state and is fresh, under 3 activity polls old. When the loop
ends, for any reason, it writes down. A file that cannot be written is logged at most
once an hour.

Stalls. orch/waits holds a line for each project that the orchestrator waits on:
the other project, since and what, split by tabs, written by wait-on. Once an activity
poll the loop reads it, and for each line the activity file of the other project, in
the home of the orchestrator account. status and wait-on read it in the same place,
so that they show the state the stall line comes from. A
file that is missing, does not parse, or is older than 3 activity polls means down,
since its written time or since the loop first saw it so. When that project has been
idle or down for the stall time, the loop appends "stall: <other> <state> since
<HH:MM> (waits on: <what>)", with the date before the time when it is not today. The
stall is over when the other project runs a unit, or is busy on 3 activity polls in a
row, so that a short turn, as one that reads an event, does not end it; the loop then
appends "resumed: <other> <state>", and a later stall gets a new stall line. Both go
through the queue in order, and neither is a change for the hourly tick. The loop
keeps what it sent in memory only, so a restarted loop can append the stall line of
the same stall once more; a cleared wait gets no further line. The stall time is
ORCH_STALL_MINUTES of <root>/host.conf, a whole number of 1 or more, or 15; a bad
value, or a file that cannot be read, is logged at the loop's start and gives 15. A
waits file or activity file that does not parse is logged at most once an hour.

Idle. The loop also wakes its own orchestrator when it sits idle while no unit runs.
An idle period starts at an activity poll with the state idle or down when none is
open, and ends at a poll with the state unit or at the third busy poll in a row, so
that the short turn that reads the event does not end it. The last end of a unit is
the time of the last poll with the state unit, or the start of the loop when there
was none. When a period has lasted the stall time and orch/waiting was not modified
after the last end of a unit, the loop appends "idle: no unit since <HH:MM>; start
the next step, or write why you wait in ~/ws/reviews/<project>/orch/waiting", once
for the period, through the queue and with no change for the hourly tick. The
orchestrator writes orch/waiting, one line with its time and the reason, when it
waits on purpose; the loop reads only its modification time, because a time that a
model writes into the line can be wrong. A missing file holds no wait.

Files, under ~/ws/reviews/<project>/: orch/events.log (the event file), orch/supervisor.log,
orch/loop.lock (the pid of the loop), orch/session-ids (the tmux session ID and the
Claude Code session ID of the session the loop started last, on one line), orch/announced, orch/reported, orch/reminded,
orch/activity, orch/waits, orch/waiting (written by the orchestrator),
orch/fetch.err (the error output of the last fetch), out/orch.txt (the tee),
transcripts/orch/<run>/. In the project's clone: the idle lines in impl/orch/updates.log.
"""

